ossible but F1 unshown count 2, less chance to complete pung.\n\nGiven W5 is dead (0 left), W4 can't form chow with W5 or W6 (W6 unshown 3 but W5 missing). W4 only use now is if we draw 2 more W4 for pung, less likely.  \nBest discard is **T7** \u2013 isolates T suit, least connected to other tiles.  \nSecond best: W4 (due to dead W5). But W4 might be slightly safer to keep for potential pung since it's not in my or opponent's discards much (only across player meld has W4).\n\nChecking opponents' discards for safety:  \nNext player has not discarded W4 or T7.  \nPrevious player hasn't discarded T7 or W4 much.  \nAcross player melded W4 but didn't discard it.  \nBut T7: next player hasn't discarded; previous player hasn't discarded; across player hasn't discarded T7. Still 4 T7 remain unshown, so not dangerous.\n\n**Winning chances**: Aim to keep honors (F2 seat/prevalent wind; F4 pair), and sequences (B2-B4 block).  \n\n**Final choice**: Discard T7.\n\n**Reason**:  \nT7 is isolated with limited chow potential given dead T9 and only moderate connections; keeping other tiles maintains better hand flexibility for chows (B2-B4), honors (F2/F4), and pairs (W3, F4). Discarding T7 minimizes damage to hand structure.\n\n---\n\nEvaluation: Discard T7 is the best among options.  \nReason: T7 is isolated with poor sequence potential in current hand; preserves B-cluster, honors, and pair possibilities.  \nAnswer: Play T7"}
